;;; An int and a string with the same text were two activators that shared
;;; one compile key — **W16**, regression `int-and-string`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`). Ein has no ints and no string values
;;; (Q-M1e2.24, Q-M1e2.23), and this file is refused at the parse.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One rule with two parameters, the relation `?R` to walk and the value
;;; `?f` to note, and two activators that differ only in their second
;;; argument, `1` and the string `"1"`. The loader held those as two values,
;;; an int and a symbol whose text is `1`, so the KB had two `holds` facts.
;;; `activator-text-string-then-int.ein` is this file with the two activator
;;; lines swapped.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, written only after `:why`, `:source`
;;; or `:goal-text` (Q-M1e2.23): a string as a value is refused at the
;;; parse, here at the first one, `(holds "1")`'s, with its file, line and
;;; column, on every command. Read as two values, each activator alone
;;; would derive its own two `noted` facts, and the two together all four.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; At `6a775f3d` the compile key rendered a non-symbol argument as text
;;; and interned the text, so both activators keyed one plan, the first
;;; one's, and the string activator compiled nothing: root held the int's
;;; two `noted` facts and not the string's (`ein solve` and `ein saturate`
;;; at `6a775f3d`: `noted 2`, one plan), and a debug build aborted in
;;; `Engine::compile_all`'s check that its shortcut skipped no activator.
;;; The compile key is the activator's values (Q-M1e.16): read as two
;;; values, the two activators would be two plans.
;;;
;;; ── What the claim can see ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The file is refused before any claim is read, so the `:expect` below is
;;; never checked. It compared facts as printed, and the fact printer drops
;;; a string's quotes, so `(noted 1 1)` and `(noted 1 "1")` were one line to
;;; it: a count of the `noted` facts told the four apart, and the claim did
;;; not.
;;;
;;;

(relation edge  Node Node)
(relation holds Node)
(relation noted Node Node)

(rule note (?R ?f)
  :match  (and (?R ?a ?b) (holds ?f))
  :assert (noted ?a ?f))

(edge 1 2) (edge 2 3)
(holds 1)  (holds "1")

(note edge 1)
(note edge "1")

(solve
  ;; `holds` and `noted` are named, so both are closed: each state lists
  ;; their complete extent, root's facts included.
  :expect (region
            ;; state 1 of 1 — ∅, root itself · minimal · maximal
            (model (holds 1) (holds "1")
                   (noted 1 1) (noted 1 "1") (noted 2 1) (noted 2 "1"))))
